{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c447","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0782c-6f74-7fc3-a546-73229f09b865"},"sequence":126,"recorded_at":1780531400000247,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d5642b5c-8a5c-4554-a722-903669c33bb0","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"d5642b5c-8a5c-4554-a722-903669c33bb0","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d5642b5c-8a5c-4554-a722-903669c33bb0"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"299292c7-0ce3-48f6-9130-4d1fdd8f9045"},"task_id":"299292c7-0ce3-48f6-9130-4d1fdd8f9045","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"299292c7-0ce3-48f6-9130-4d1fdd8f9045","chunk":"Read text file `README.md`.\n1|# Agent Session Manager (`ax`)\n2|\n3|Agent Session Manager is the implementation repository for the local-first,\n4|cross-host `ax` coding-agent session orchestrator.\n5|\n6|## Project Status\n7|\n8|The Curator-managed repository foundation is complete and the published AX\n9|v0.5.0 specification has been decomposed into an implementation board. The\n10|Go contract foundation pins the immutable normative source and generates typed\n11|contract, operation, capability-vocabulary, event, and error catalogs from\n12|reviewed implementation metadata. The local storage foundation also resolves\n13|the five Section 3.2 path classes and installs verified immutable blobs on\n14|native macOS/Linux filesystems. Its local SQLite index projects those supported\n15|raw blobs without becoming authority. Session, provider, terminal, mesh, and\n16|operator command behavior is not implemented or advertised by this slice.\n17|\n18|Product behavior is defined by the\n19|[normative AX specification](https://github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec/blob/main/SPEC.md).\n20|When this repository and the specification disagree, the specification is\n21|authoritative.\n22|\n23|## Bootstrap\n24|\n25|The machine used for repository bootstrap runs Curator\n26|`v0.14.0-rc.3-106-g665dd13f`, built from `relux-works/curator` main revision\n27|`665dd13f7b6ab0408300070ef68d895f2d03b79f`.\n28|\n29|From a fresh checkout, verify Curator, install the exact project-local skill\n30|revisions and agent adapters pinned in `Skillfile.json`, and check the managed\n31|state:\n32|\n33|```bash\n34|curator --version\n35|curator install\n36|curator status --check\n37|```\n38|\n39|Configure the required repository-local Git identity and SSH signing policy:\n40|\n41|```bash\n42|git config --local user.name \"Ivan Oparin\"\n43|git config --local user.email \"oparin@me.com\"\n44|git config --local gpg.format ssh\n45|git config --local user.signingkey /Users/iv/.ssh/ivanopcode.pub\n46|git config --local commit.gpgsign true\n47|git config --local tag.gpgsign true\n48|```\n49|\n50|Contributor and agent workflow rules live in [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md).\n51|`CLAUDE.md` is a relative symlink to that canonical file.\n52|\n53|## Implementation Plan\n54|\n55|The live `task-board` contains five implementation milestones (`M0` through\n56|`M4`) with 62 Stories and 186 atomic agent-executable Tasks. The dependency\n57|closure of the final release Task contains every implementation Task, and the\n58|machine-derived milestone critical path is:\n59|\n60|```text\n61|M0 contract foundation\n62|  -> M1 single-host durability\n63|  -> M2 multi-host preview\n64|  -> M3 daily-driver tmux\n65|  -> M4 cloning, Directory, platforms, and product release\n66|```\n67|\n68|All optional human UX, pilot, fidelity, and product go/no-go work is isolated\n69|in a separate advisory Epic with no hard dependency into the implementation\n70|DAG. It cannot block autonomous agent execution.\n71|\n72|See [.spec/README.md](.spec/README.md) for the pinned specification source,\n73|section coverage, board IDs, counts, and execution rules. Generated planning\n74|snapshots live in [.planning/](.planning/); the live task-board remains the\n75|planning authority.\n76|\n77|## Normative Source Pin\n78|\n79|[`internal/specpin`](internal/specpin) embeds one implementation lock for\n80|`agent-session-manager-spec@v0.5.0`. Its production `Current` and `Verify`\n81|entry points fail closed on a partial read, unknown member, source substitution,\n82|contract or fixture drift, and any byte-level lock change. It also projects the\n83|exact historical v0.4.3 registry without widening that immutable baseline.\n84|\n85|This package is read-only and does not mutate durable state. Repeated reads are\n86|idempotent and isolated from caller mutation. It deliberately adds no `ax`\n87|command, `doctor` result, conformance-target declaration, or runtime capability\n88|claim; those surfaces remain unavailable until their owning implementation and\n89|acceptance tasks land.\n90|\n91|## Pinned Specification Document\n92|\n93|[`internal/specdoc`](internal/specdoc) embeds the byte-exact `SPEC.md` of that\n94|same pinned release so repository fidelity gates can compare their artifacts\n95|against the specification text instead of against the implementation those\n96|artifacts are supposed to constrain. The upstream specification remains\n97|normative; this copy is a verification input and amends nothing.\n98|\n99|`Load` accepts the document only when its SHA-256 equals\n100|`specpin.DocumentSHA256`, so a substituted, edited, truncated, or unreadable\n101|document is refused rather than silently compared against — otherwise a swapped\n102|specification would confirm whatever it happened to contain. `QuoteLines`\n103|resolves an excerpt to the 1-based `SPEC.md` lines it begins on, `SectionID`\n104|resolves a line to the numbered clause that contains it, `TableRowAt` reports\n105|what a Markdown table body row declares, and all three work under one\n106|normalization rule: runs of ASCII whitespace collapse to a single space. Case,\n107|punctuation, digits, and inline `<code>` markup are compared exactly. A\n108|whitespace run that crosses a hard boundary collapses instead to a block\n109|separator that no normalized excerpt can contain, so a quote cannot stitch\n110|across one; both halves of such a stitch are individually verbatim, so\n111|whitespace collapsing alone admitted it. Two boundaries are hard: a blank line,\n112|which separates a block from the next, and the newline between two adjacent\n113|table rows, because a table row is a complete line by construction. Inside one\n114|block the newline is still forgiven, deliberately — the document's hard line\n115|wrapping, its table indentation, and the newline between two adjacent list items\n116|or two adjacent lines of one paragraph.\n117|\n118|Only repository gates import this package - the test binaries that check\n119|enumeration artifacts, and `internal/traceability`, which measures the clause\n120|inventory of a bound section from it. The embedded document never reaches the\n121|`ax` command, which does not exist yet, and\n122|`TestEmbeddedDocumentNeverReachesAProductBinary` is what keeps that true once it\n123|does: it reads the module import graph from source, refuses any `main` package\n124|outside `tracecheck` that can reach this package, and proves the detector by\n125|planting a `cmd/ax` that imports `internal/traceability`. It is read-only,\n126|mutates no durable state, and advertises no runtime capability.\n127|\n128|## Common Wire Scalar Types\n129|\n130|[`internal/scalar`](internal/scalar) implements the validated AX value types\n131|used by later schema owners: canonical lowercase UUIDv4 and UUIDv7 values, real\n132|UTC RFC3339 timestamps with millisecond-or-finer precision, SHA-256 digest\n133|identifiers, the current four-member AX platform vocabulary, bounded provider\n134|IDs, platform-neutral relative paths, platform-bound absolute paths, safe and\n135|bounded JSON integers, `decimal_uint64`, and exact lower-snake-case closed\n136|enums. Embedded Git values use the same production scalar layer for exact\n137|SHA-1/SHA-256 OIDs, fully qualified `git check-ref-format` references, and\n138|sanitized HTTPS/SSH/git remote URLs that contain no password or token, query,\n139|fragment, local path, or unsupported scheme.\n140|\n141|Timestamp validation accepts published UTC leap seconds, including\n142|`1990-12-31T23:59:60.000Z`, while refusing a fabricated `:60` in an ordinary\n143|minute or on an unpublished date. Because Go's `time.Time` has no leap-second\n144|representation, `Timestamp.Time` maps an accepted leap second to the following\n145|representable UTC second while the scalar retains the original wire text.\n146|\n147|Constructors and JSON/text decoders fail closed on malformed, null,\n148|out-of-range, wrong-version, traversal, encoded-separator, wrong-platform,\n149|unknown-enum, or fabricated leap-second inputs. `AbsolutePath` decoding\n150|requires the containing platform, and generic closed-enum decoding requires\n151|the exact negotiated vocabulary; neither guesses that context from a field\n152|name or value prefix. Native Windows paths additionally refuse Win32-reserved\n153|component punctuation, control characters, and DOS device names even when a\n154|device name has an extension; those Windows-only component rules do not alter\n155|POSIX path validation. These types are read-only value validation. They do not\n156|mutate durable state and add no `ax doctor` result, provider/platform\n157|availability claim, or runtime capability advertisement.\n158|\n159|`FuzzScalarProductionEntries` drives the production constructors and wire\n160|decoders with a checked-in corpus covering published and fabricated leap\n161|seconds, lowercase RFC3339, impossible dates, malformed identifiers, unsafe\n162|integer boundaries, and Windows reserved-device and wildcard components. Any\n163|accepted value must publish and read back byte-for-byte without a later\n164|refusal.\n165|\n166|Run the focused tests and assigned-scope gate with:\n167|\n168|```bash\n169|go test ./internal/scalar -count=1\n170|go test ./internal/scalar -cover -count=1\n171|go test ./internal/scalar -run=^$ \\\n172|  -fuzz=^FuzzScalarProductionEntries$ -fuzztime=100x -parallel=1\n173|go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck\n174|```\n175|\n176|The assigned-scope gate refuses Sections 1.6, 10.1, 10.2, 10.3 and 10.4: each\n177|binding validates a corner of its section and the gate now says so with the\n178|measured ratio. See [Specification-to-Code Ownership\n179|Gate](#specification-to-code-ownership-gate).\n180|\n181|```bash\n182|go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck \\\n183|  -section 1.6 -section 10.1 -section 10.2 -section 10.3 -section 10.4\n184|# exits non-zero: 0/31, 0/3, 0/5, 1/3 and 0/25 normative clauses discharged\n185|```\n186|\n187|## Configuration Loading and Versioned Schemas\n188|\n189|The internal/config package implements the read-only Section 3.2 path-selection\n190|boundary used by Section 6.1 configuration loading. LoadOS captures the\n191|caller-supplied runtime-probed AX platform and current OS inputs once; Load is\n192|the dependency-injected production entry used by deterministic tests and\n193|alternate launchers.\n194|\n195|One five-row registry drives --config, --data-dir, --state-dir, --cache-dir,\n196|and --runtime-dir together with their exact AX_* counterparts. Resolution\n197|applies flag, non-empty documented environment override, then platform default\n198|independently to every path class. Relative flag and override values are made\n199|absolute against the captured working directory before use. XDG, macOS,\n200|WSL2/Linux, and native Windows defaults use native path grammar; missing\n201|required Linux runtime or Windows profile inputs fail closed.\n202|\n203|A failed user-home lookup is deferred rather than refused at capture time,\n204|because Windows derives no path default from the user home and several classes\n205|are satisfied by an XDG override. The captured cause travels into the\n206|platform-default refusal of every home-derived class, so an operator still sees\n207|why the default was unavailable. Both halves are proven at the real LoadOS and\n208|MigrateOS entries against a home the case makes and unmakes through the exact\n209|environment variable os.UserHomeDir reads; no case assigns the captured value or\n210|its error directly.\n211|\n212|Load returns isolated TOML bytes, one immutable resolved-root snapshot, and an\n213|isolated current-model Configuration when the selected file exists. It dispatches\n214|strict closed-table readers for Configuration 1.0.0, 2.0.0, and 3.0.0 from the\n215|pinned catalog; v1/v2 are translated in memory to v3 without changing their\n216|source bytes. `EncodeCurrent` validates and emits only Configuration 3.0.0.\n217|Backend-specific v3 settings require an exact caller-supplied settings validator;\n218|without one, a `backend_config` entry fails closed rather than accepting an\n219|arbitrary blob. Only an enabled external-trust entry registers its backend for\n220|selection or backend-specific configuration; disabled entries remain\n221|round-trippable but cannot authorize activation. An explicit empty\n222|`transport_policy` is the valid deny-all subset, distinct from omission, whose\n223|default is both closed transport names.\n224|\n225|A not-yet-created configuration file is accepted only when its parent directory\n226|exists, and ConfigPresent distinguishes that state from an existing empty\n227|file. Versioned validation applies closed members, scalar and relational bounds,\n228|unique/sorted registries, SSH host-authentication refusals, directory disclosure\n229|constraints, and the v3 TerminalBackend trust/config shape from Sections 6 and\n230|17. Required closed map members preserve the distinction between absence and an\n231|explicit empty object in both inline (`extensions = {}`) and multiline table\n232|syntax, and current-version writer output round-trips through the reader. Legacy\n233|`terminal.backend` accepts exactly `tmux|conpty` before translation.\n234|The v3 terminal capability vocabulary is derived from the pinned catalog's\n235|`terminal_backend` family, and the supported reader set is tested as a derived\n236|property of the pinned Configuration catalog, so neither a new capability nor a\n237|new Configuration version can silently lack enforcement. Full package tests also\n238|derive the production refusal-site inventory from source and fail when a new\n239|configuration refusal has no exercised negative path. That derivation proves\n240|its own coverage: it enumerates every error type the package declares, requires\n241|exactly one instrumented constructor per type, and reports any refusal built\n242|outside that constructor, so a second refusal form or a duplicated constructor\n243|cannot report green. Sites excluded as subsumed are pinned by an explicit\n244|inventory test and each names the check that covers it. An omitted\n245|`required_capabilities` remains marked as contextual default provenance; this\n246|package does not invent a platform lane minimum that only activation evidence\n247|can establish.\n248|\n249|`Migrate` and `MigrateOS` are the explicit Section 6.4/6.5 durable migration\n250|entries; ordinary `Load` and startup never call them. Migration validates the\n251|selected source first, requires the v1 generated-summary disclosure choice,\n252|re-inspects the selected file's kind and permission bits before anything durable\n253|is written, creates an owner-only versioned backup containing the exact old\n254|bytes, writes\n255|and fsyncs a same-directory temporary file, atomically replaces the source,\n256|and fsyncs the directory. Exact backups are safely reusable on retry, a\n257|pre-existing backup whose bytes differ from the source, whose mode is readable\n258|beyond the owner, or that cannot be read at all is refused rather than treated\n259|as satisfied, an already-target-version source is a no-op, and a post-replace\n260|directory-sync failure restores the old bytes before returning an error. A\n261|failure injected at each durable step leaves the selected file exactly as it\n262|was, and a rollback that itself fails is reported as a recovery failure rather\n263|than an ordinary sync failure. Every file the migration stages is fsynced before\n264|it becomes visible, not only the last one, and the replacement inherits the\n265|selected file's exact permission bits rather than widening or tightening them.\n266|Migration retains every Configuration 1.0.0 member: the member set is derived\n267|from the versioned wire type and a source that carries every member at a\n268|non-default value is compared as a whole loaded `Configuration` before and after\n269|each supported target, so a member an encoder drops cannot decay silently to its\n270|default. `AssessCompatibility`\n271|reads only the schema envelope and reports `read-only-diagnostic` when a reader\n272|is older than the document; it exposes no decoded configuration or writer path,\n273|and the mode is pinned across the full cross-product of pinned Configuration\n274|versions, so the one-major step is asserted alongside the two-major one.\n275|\n276|These are package entry points, not an advertised `ax migrate config` command\n277|or `doctor` result. The package still does not enumerate environment variables,\n278|copy ambient credentials, interpret unknown AX_* names, mutate roots, or claim\n279|runtime capability/backend availability. Existing selected roots are checked\n280|for directory kind, while absent roots remain eligible for\n281|the later owner-only initialization boundary. An absent config with a missing\n282|or non-directory parent, a wrong existing file kind, a read failure, and a root\n283|inspection failure remain distinct through `errors.Is`; rendered loader errors\n284|omit wrapped OS/filesystem details so selected machine-local paths cannot leak.\n285|\n286|The two seams take deliberately different symlink stances, and both are pinned\n287|in both directions at their real `LoadOS` and `MigrateOS` entries. The read seam\n288|resolves symlinks and then applies the Section 3.2 value kind to the resolved\n289|target: a configuration file symlinked onto a regular file loads, a root\n290|symlinked onto a directory loads, and either link pointed at the wrong kind\n291|still fails closed with the same typed refusal a direct path would produce. The\n292|pinned specification states no no-follow requirement for these five classes and\n293|resolves symlinks before comparison where it speaks about them at all, so\n294|refusing an ordinary dotfiles or Application Support symlink would be an\n295|invented constraint; a dangling configuration symlink accordingly resolves to a\n296|not-yet-created regular-file path whose parent exists, which `Load` admits\n297|without creating anything. The durable migration seam does not follow symlinks,\n298|because its atomic rename would replace the operator's link rather than the\n299|document the link points at, silently detaching it and leaving the real document\n300|holding stale bytes. The specification is silent about that mutation, so the\n301|mutating path fails closed: a selected file that is not itself a regular file is\n302|refused with `ErrConfigNotRegular` before any backup, staging file, or\n303|replacement is written, and the refusal leaves the directory exactly as it was.\n304|\n305|Section 6.3 requires refusing `StrictHostKeyChecking=no`, an empty\n306|`UserKnownHostsFile`, \"or an equivalent host-authentication bypass\" in a mesh\n307|peer's `ssh_args`. An equivalence class has no enumeration, so admission is\n308|derived rather than blacklisted: `internal/config/sshargs.go` declares the\n309|`ssh(1)` short-option arity transcribed from the OpenSSH usage text, the short\n310|options AX admits, and the `-o` option registry with the values each option\n311|permits. Every argument outside that declaration is refused, so an option name\n312|the parser has never heard of — `ProxyCommand`, `KnownHostsCommand`, `Include`,\n313|`LocalCommand`, or anything OpenSSH adds later — fails closed, as do `-F`, a\n314|bare destination or remote command, and any grouped short flag carrying them.\n315|Short options are walked with getopt semantics, so `-vo StrictHostKeyChecking=no`\n316|and `-4o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null` are seen as the options OpenSSH resolves\n317|them to. `StrictHostKeyChecking` is declared with only its enforcing spelling,\n318|which refuses the live `false` alias without listing aliases. The refusal clause\n319|distinguishes a host-authentication option from an undeclared name, an\n320|unpermitted flag, and an unpermitted value; the negative suite derives its cases\n321|from those same tables, so a newly declared option is covered the moment it is\n322|added.\n323|\n324|That gate reads `ssh_args` only, and Section 6.2 hands `ssh(1)` the peer\n325|`endpoint` as an atomic argv value too. An atomic argv value is not by itself a\n326|destination: `ssh(1)` reads its destination positionally through getopt, so an\n327|endpoint beginning with `-` is parsed as an option. `-oStrictHostKeyChecking=no`\n328|written into `endpoint` was therefore the same Section 6.3 bypass reached\n329|through a field the `ssh_args` gate never inspects, and an endpoint carrying a\n330|space is that injection one word-split away. `internal/config/endpoint.go`\n331|closes it the same derived way: the field is admitted against a closed\n332|`[user@]host[:port]` grammar — a 1-64 character login name, LDH DNS labels or a\n333|bracketed IPv6 literal, and a decimal port in 1-65535 — and refused otherwise,\n334|naming which clause it violated. The grammar is narrower than every destination\n335|`ssh(1)` would accept, so widening it is a reviewed change; the 1-1024 character\n336|endpoint bound stays reachable through the grammar and is still proved at both\n337|its limits.\n338|\n339|Every negative case for that grammar carries an isolating neighbour: the same\n340|endpoint with only the named violation removed, asserted to be admitted. A case\n341|that names the `host` clause but would also be refused by the `port` clause\n342|pins nothing, so the neighbour is what proves the named clause is the one\n343|deciding. The leading-hyphen clause needs it most — the reported shapes such as\n344|`-oStrictHostKeyChecking=no` are refused by the host grammar too once the\n345|hyphen clause is gone, while `-ivan@peer.example` is not, because `-` is a legal\n346|login-name byte and `ssh(1)` reads that value as `-i` with identity file\n347|`van@peer.example`.\n348|\n349|Section 6 states one rule for an `extensions` key: it is a reverse-DNS key of\n350|3-253 lowercase ASCII characters with at least one dot and dot-separated labels\n351|matching `[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}`. No label is reserved, so `validateExtensions`\n352|admits or refuses a key by that grammar and the 253-byte bound alone, and an\n353|`ExtensionValue` object is constrained only to string keys within nesting depth\n354|4. A reverse-DNS namespace this organisation owns — `works.relux.env-tools`,\n355|`com.example.auth-manager`, `io.example.endpoint-list` — therefore loads, and a\n356|nested key named `endpoint` or `token` is preserved as data. Reading a name as\n357|evidence about a value is not secret detection: the only \"secret, token,\n358|endpoint credential\" clause in Section 6 governs a terminal backend-config\n359|`settings` object, and it is enforced where it applies, by the closed schema a\n360|backend implementation version registers, which refuses any settings object it\n361|did not declare. Section 6.4's \"no v2 table accepts a secret, endpoint\n362|credential, model token, auth root, or arbitrary environment passthrough\" is\n363|likewise a field-declaration rule, enforced by the closed table shape\n364|`decodeStrict` decodes with `DisallowUnknownFields`; Section 6.1 states the\n365|value rule and its permission together, forbidding secret *values* in config\n366|fields while allowing a provider to name a machine-local environment variable or\n367|credential profile.\n368|\n369|Run the focused tests and assigned-scope traceability gate with:\n370|\n371|    go test ./internal/config -count=1 -v\n372|    go test ./internal/config -cover -count=1\n373|    go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck -section 6.2\n374|\n375|Section 6.2 is the only configuration section the assigned-scope gate admits:\n376|its single normative clause is enumerated and discharged. Sections 3.2, 6.1,\n377|6.3, 6.4, 6.5, 17.1, 17.2 and 17.4 are refused with their measured ratio, and\n378|Section 6.5 additionally names the `required_capabilities` default defect. See\n379|[Specification-to-Code Ownership Gate](#specification-to-code-ownership-gate).\n380|\n381|## Provider Plugin Discovery and Trust\n382|\n383|[`internal/provider`](internal/provider) implements the Section 7.1 trusted\n384|provider plugin host boundary: deterministic executable discovery, trust\n385|recording, and substitution detection. `Discover` is the production entry\n386|point. It enumerates candidates in the section's source order — configured\n387|`providers.plugin_dirs` in listed order (entry names sorted bytewise within\n388|each directory), then the six built-in adapters (`codex`, `claude`, `gemini`,\n389|`muse`, `antigravity`, `pi`), then `PATH` only when `allow_path_plugins` is\n390|true — and records the canonical absolute path, SHA-256 digest, and approving\n391|owner for every accepted external `ax-provider-<id>` executable. Symlinks are\n392|resolved before comparison, the target must be a regular file owned by the\n393|operator or an administrator-approved identity, and `Trust`/`Verify` carry the\n394|receipt a changed path target or digest must renew.\n395|\n396|If two candidates declare the same provider ID, discovery fails with\n397|`invalid_config` before either is probed or executed; the package has no probe\n398|or execution dependency, and no source imports a process-execution facility, so\n399|the ordering holds structurally. Filesystem read failures abort discovery with\n400|`local_precondition_failed` instead of yielding a partial set, and a receipt\n401|that no longer matches freshly read facts fails verification with\n402|`integrity_failure`. All three codes are pinned against the contract registry\n403|with their exact exits (3, 3, 9). A discovered candidate carries no\n404|availability, status, or capability claim, and the package is an internal M0\n405|host boundary, not a public stable plugin SDK, which the section defers. The\n406|package mutates no durable state: the host persists the `TrustRecord`, and\n407|`Verify` rechecks it. Native Windows owner attestation is unimplemented, so\n408|external executables are undiscoverable there until a Windows owner model\n409|lands; built-in adapters are unaffected.\n410|\n411|Run the focused tests and coverage with:\n412|\n413|```bash\n414|go test ./internal/provider -count=1\n415|go test ./internal/provider -cover -count=1\n416|```\n417|\n418|The package derives its refusal inventory from its own source: every refusal\n419|constructor call site must have an exercised negative path, no `Error` value\n420|may be built outside the four constructors, and the observed code set must\n421|equal the closed three-code set. Section 7.1 citations in its tests resolve\n422|against the digest-pinned document in `internal/specdoc`, quoting the exact\n423|line each claim begins on.\n424|\n425|## Provider Plugin JSONL Protocol\n426|\n427|[`internal/provhost`](internal/provhost) implements the ax host side of\n428|the Section 7.2 provider plugin JSON-over-stdio protocol: one-frame JSONL\n429|transport, deadlines, size limits, stdout/stderr separation, operation\n430|dispatch, and status recovery. `Host.Call` is the production entry point.\n431|It frames one request envelope (`protocol`, `protocol_version`,\n432|`request_id`, `operation`, `deadline`, `body`), starts one plugin process\n433|per operation, enforces the request deadline, and interprets the single\n434|response frame. Unknown operations, stale deadlines, and unframeable\n435|bodies are refused with `invalid_config` before any process starts.\n436|\n437|Each stdout line must be one complete UTF-8 JSON object no larger than 8\n438|MiB; stdout carries the single response frame while diagnostics stay on\n439|stderr and never enter failure text. A recognizable foreign major yields\n440|`incompatible_protocol` without trusting the payload; every other\n441|unusable frame yields `provider_protocol_error`, both as local Structured\n442|Error 1.0.0 objects built without adopting any child-supplied code,\n443|retryable bit, details, or authority. A plugin that exceeds its deadline\n444|is terminated (as a process group on unix; direct-child kill on Windows)\n445|and reported as `provider_timeout`; a\n446|crash without a response is `provider_process_failed`. On success the raw\n447|body is returned for the operation layer; on failure the bound child\n448|error is returned itself. All six codes are pinned against the contract\n449|registry with their exact exits (3, 13, 6, 13, 13, 9).\n450|\n451|Dispatch covers exactly the 15 Section 7.5 operations in manifest order,\n452|derived from the pinned specification text, and refuses any other name\n453|without spawning. Status recovery interprets `materialize-status` bodies\n454|through the Section 7.5 state rules: unknown fails closed with\n455|`integrity_failure` for quarantine, prepared requires plan, token, and\n456|discovery, terminal states require plan and discovery with no token, and\n457|identity members must equal the requested transaction. Reads evolve —\n458|prepared under one envelope may be committed under the next — and each\n459|call starts a fresh process, so recovery observes durable state through\n460|the passed authority with no host-side mutation cache.\n461|\n462|The operation layer validates what a plugin returned: the closed\n463|Section 7.3 manifest (registries derived from the pinned example),\n464|the Section 7.4 probe with its seven capability values (only\n465|`available` may be `enabled`), the host-side capability gate\n466|(`RequireCapability` refuses unproven surfaces before any process\n467|starts), the exact Section 7.7 profile mapping (six providers times\n468|two profiles swept, standard always the empty omission), the\n469|Section 7.6 quiescence proof (a `safe` claim over any unproven fact\n470|is refused; honest `unsafe` validates), the launch/resume SpawnPlan\n471|(argv, destination-native cwd, sorted unique environment names,\n472|literals disjoint from inherited names, and a `profile_mapping`\n473|equal to the Section 7.7 mapping), the Section 5.5 Provider Identity\n474|Record with the identify-session wrapper, and the single\n475|`(operation, operation_id)` mutation key (keyed operations derived\n476|per-row from the Section 7.5 table). A lost mutation retried with\n477|identical bytes returns byte-identical results across fresh\n478|processes; a changed body surfaces the bound `idempotency_mismatch`\n479|child with no further frame sent. Mutation receipts themselves live\n480|in the transaction document and the plugin, not the host. Full\n481|Section 7.5 request-body vocabularies beyond these surfaces, and the\n482|materialize commit/rollback result bodies, still cross the package\n483|opaquely behind the object-shape check.\n484|\n485|Run the focused tests and coverage with:\n486|\n487|```bash\n488|go test ./internal/provhost -count=1\n489|go test ./internal/provhost -cover -count=1\n490|```\n491|\n492|The package derives its refusal inventory from its own source: every\n493|refusal constructor call site must have an exercised negative path, no\n494|Structured Error may be built outside the six constructors, no raw error\n495|may be minted, and the observed code set must equal the closed six-code\n496|set. Every refusal assertion names the arm it must reach (the refused\n497|member and the rule detail), so a gate deleted from a required-member list\n498|slides to a lower arm and reddens instead of passing silently. The\n499|rollback-token entropy floor is pinned at exactly 31 refused / 32 accepted\n500|decoded bytes, both frame-size bounds are pinned at exactly limit refused /\n(more lines below)"}}}
