{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4dd","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0782c-6f74-7fc3-a546-73229f09b865"},"sequence":201,"recorded_at":1780531400000397,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d5642b5c-8a5c-4554-a722-903669c33bb0","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"d5642b5c-8a5c-4554-a722-903669c33bb0","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d5642b5c-8a5c-4554-a722-903669c33bb0"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"28738699-0b44-422b-b274-11342429d1a2"},"task_id":"28738699-0b44-422b-b274-11342429d1a2","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"28738699-0b44-422b-b274-11342429d1a2","chunk":"Read text file `README.md`.\n(699 line(s) above)\n700|## Continuous Conformance Gates (CI)\n701|\n702|`.github/workflows/ci.yml` is the gate that decides what a green check\n703|means. Every job proves its selection matched something: a test selector,\n704|file scan, derivation, or matrix expansion that finds nothing fails closed\n705|instead of passing on an empty result set. No job declares a skip\n706|condition, so no job can skip quietly; the terminal verdict job additionally\n707|refuses any skipped, failed, or cancelled dependency.\n708|\n709|| Job | What runs | What green proves |\n710|| --- | --- | --- |\n711|| Contract preservation | `tracecheck`, catalog freshness, focused `internal/cigate` contract gates | The pinned lock and the generated catalog agree on every historical and current contract version, and each gate selection matched its named test |\n712|| Linters | `go vet`, Windows `go vet`, `gofmt` with a non-empty-scan guard | The tree type-checks on both GOOS values and carries no formatting drift |\n713|| Test | Full suite uncached | Every package passed; the run matched at least one package |\n714|| Race detector | Full suite under `-race` | No data race fired anywhere in the suite |\n715|| Coverage numbers | Full suite with `-cover`, percentages in the run summary | Per-package coverage was measured and reported; there is no floor, so coverage is a number, not a gate |\n716|| Conformance fixtures | Story suites on Ubuntu and macOS | Exact fixtures and negative/refusal cases pass per platform, with skip lines and the probe-counter line as evidence; crash/idempotency evidence comes from the Transactor battery in this matrix |\n717|| Fuzz smoke | Targets derived from the compiled test binary, each smoked briefly | Every derived target executed its engine, proven by its elapsed line rather than by exit status alone |\n718|| Unsupported-capability claim check | README scanned against live probe verdicts | No unsupported capability is advertised: mentions resolve to probe, skip, gate, or test context, never to a positive availability claim about an unavailable capability |\n719|| Windows compile-only | `GOOS=windows` build and vet, test files included | The tree compiles for Windows; green here never implies behaviour was executed there |\n720|| Gates verdict | Dependency-result check | Every gate above succeeded and none skipped |\n721|\n722|[`internal/cigate`](internal/cigate) holds the CI-facing gate library:\n723|contract-version agreement derived from `internal/specpin` and\n724|`internal/catalog` (a version either side cannot account for is refused),\n725|fuzz-target derivation from the instrument's own target map, and the\n726|advertisement check wired to the `internal/secconftest` probe set rather\n727|than to a second vocabulary. It ships no command by deliberation: its\n728|library reaches the embedded specification document through the\n729|instrument, which the `specdoc` guard reserves to the existing repository\n730|gates, so CI drives these gates through focused name-guarded test\n731|selections whose guards prove the selection matched.\n732|\n733|## Canonical JSON and Immutable Object Identities\n734|\n735|[`internal/canonicaljson`](internal/canonicaljson) exposes the production RFC\n736|8785 JCS entry point and the AX immutable-object identity calculation. The JCS\n737|path validates I-JSON input before canonicalization, including duplicate-name,\n738|UTF-8, surrogate-pair, ECMAScript number-formatting, string-escaping, and\n739|UTF-16 property-ordering rules. Its number behavior is checked against every\n740|finite RFC 8785 Appendix B sample through the production entry point.\n741|\n742|All four Section 1.6 boundary fixtures that publish a digest are pinned:\n743|`NUM-SAFE-MAX`, `NUM-U64-STRING`, `NUM-U64-MAX`, and `JCS-UTF16-ORDER` are each\n744|recomputed from the production encoder and compared against the value quoted\n745|from `SPEC.md` at the pinned commit, not against a value the implementation\n746|derived for itself.\n747|\n748|The `utf8.ValidString` re-checks in `closed_shapes.go` are kept and documented\n749|rather than deleted; each names `decodeStrict` as the validator that subsumes it.\n750|That subsumption is machine-checked, not only asserted in prose: no exported\n751|function or method may hand an already-decoded value to one of those re-checks,\n752|and `decodeStrict` must remain the only place in the package where bytes become\n753|Go values. Adding a second decoder reddens the pin, as does a map-taking\n754|exported entry point that reaches a re-check by calling it, through the\n755|package's own `immutableObjectShapeValidators` dispatch table, or as a method on\n756|an exported type. All 7 of the 7 derived re-check functions are reachable from\n757|an exported entry point, so none of them is pinned vacuously, and that ratio is\n758|itself asserted rather than left to prose.\n759|\n(more lines below)"}}}
